{
  "slug": "qpadm-denisova-two-neanderthal-sources",
  "title": "qpAdm: Denisova modelled from two Neanderthals",
  "summary": "Two-source qpAdm fit for Denisova.DG; p = 0.028, verdict infeasible.",
  "kind": "qpadm",
  "result_kind": "formal",
  "tags": [
    "qpadm",
    "formal",
    "archaic",
    "demonstration",
    "two-source"
  ],
  "description": "Models Denisova.DG as a mixture of Altai Neanderthal and Vindija relative to four deep outgroups. This is the worked example from ADMIXTOOLS 2's own qpadm() documentation, so the output can be checked directly against the upstream package. It is a useful demonstration of what a rejected model looks like: Denisovans are a sister lineage to Neanderthals, not a mixture of two of them, so the fit statistic is expected to be poor. Read the rank-drop table and the weight standard errors, not just the point estimates.",
  "caveats": [
    "Computed from admixtools::example_f2_blocks, the f2-statistics bundled with ADMIXTOOLS 2 for seven populations (Altai Neanderthal, Vindija, Denisova, Mbuti, Chimp, Russia_Ust_Ishim, Switzerland_Bichon). These are real data and the statistics are genuine, but they are the package's demonstration populations -- archaic hominins and Palaeolithic modern humans. This is NOT a South Asian analysis and must not be presented as one. South Asian examples require the AADR reference panel built by data/pipeline/.",
    "qpAdm does not identify the true history. A model that is not rejected is consistent with these data given this outgroup set; other untested models may fit as well or better. p-values depend on the right populations, the SNP set and the jackknife block size."
  ],
